---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import GuideLayout from '../../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';

import shotInbox from '../../../assets/guide/07-inbox.png';
import shotInboxDetail from '../../../assets/guide/08-inbox-detail.png';
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="referral-inbox"
  title="Work the referral inbox"
  description="Every concern raised at your school lands in one queue, ordered so the most urgent and longest-waiting cases sit at the top."
  audience="For counsellors"
  readingTime="4 min read"
>
  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Open Referrals</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Referrals</strong> in the sidebar is your queue. It covers every school whose
        roster you are on, so a counsellor working across two schools sees both in one place.
      </p>
      <p>The three counts at the top are the ones worth watching:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Unclaimed</strong>: nobody has taken these on yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Urgent</strong>: a teacher marked these as needing attention now.</li>
        <li><strong>In progress</strong>: cases somebody is actively working.</li>
      </ul>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotInbox}
          alt="The referral inbox showing three cases: Ananya Iyer flagged by self-assessment and marked urgent, Aarav Sharma referred by a teacher, and Kabir Menon already in progress with a counsellor assigned."
          loading="eager"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Sorted urgent first, then longest-waiting, so the top of the list is always the next thing
          to look at.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Tell the two kinds of case apart</h2>
      <p>Cases arrive by two routes, and the difference matters.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A teacher referral</strong> names the teacher who filed it. Someone has noticed
          something in class and written down what they saw.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A self-assessment flag</strong> carries a <strong>Self-assessment</strong> chip
          and no teacher. The student's own check-in scored past the distress threshold and raised
          the case automatically.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="guide-note guide-privacy">
        <p>
          <strong>Self-assessment flags are invisible to teachers.</strong> They never appear on a
          class roster or in any teacher's list. The student's own screening result is between them
          and you, so when you follow one up, be aware the student's teachers do not know it exists.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Narrow the queue</h2>
      <p>
        The filters along the top cut the list down by status, by who it is assigned to, and by
        urgency. <strong>Unacknowledged</strong> is the useful one at the start of a day: it is
        everything nobody has picked up.
      </p>
      <p>
        The search box matches on student name within the schools you are rostered to, and nothing
        beyond them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Open a case and read it</h2>
      <p>
        Click any row. The panel shows the student, their class, who referred them and when, the
        concerns tagged, and the referring teacher's description in their own words.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a self-assessment flag you get the screening snapshot instead: the instrument used, the
        score, and the threshold it crossed. That snapshot is frozen, so a later retake does not
        rewrite the evidence you acted on.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={shotInboxDetail}
          alt="A referral opened in the side panel, showing the class, the referring teacher, the quoted description of what the teacher observed, the concern tags, and a Claim button."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          Opening a case is recorded, so the school can always answer who looked at a student's
          record and when.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Claim it</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Claim</strong> assigns the case to you and moves it to acknowledged. From that
        point it is clearly yours, and it drops out of everyone else's unclaimed count.
      </p>
      <div class="guide-note">
        <p>
          <strong>Two counsellors cannot claim the same case.</strong> If a colleague got there
          first, you will be told who holds it rather than silently taking it from them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <div class="step-number">6</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <h2>Move it along, and close it properly</h2>
      <p>
        Set the case to <strong>In progress</strong> once you are working with the student. When you
        close it, you record an outcome and you get two separate note fields. The difference between
        them is the important part of this screen.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Closure note</strong>: yours. Clinical detail, kept on the case. No teacher ever
          sees this.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Summary for the teacher</strong>: short, and written knowing the referring teacher
          will read it. Enough for them to keep supporting the student in class, and no more.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="guide-note guide-privacy">
        <p>
          <strong>Leave the teacher summary blank and the teacher gets nothing.</strong> That is a
          valid choice when there is genuinely nothing shareable, but a teacher who filed a careful
          referral and hears nothing back tends to stop filing them. A single sentence is usually
          the right amount.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-footer">
    <h2>Things worth knowing</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>One open case per student per school.</strong> If two teachers notice the same child,
      they converge on one thread rather than creating duplicates you would have to reconcile.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A student who changes class stays on your case.</strong> The case is flagged with the
      move so you know it happened, but it does not jump to another counsellor underneath you.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A referral is not an emergency channel.</strong> Even an urgent one is a queue. If a
      student is in immediate danger, the school's emergency protocol is what applies.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stuck? Email <a href="mailto:admin@zengarden.co.in">admin@zengarden.co.in</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
